From 7c96181c6219282ca3bb4c5ca678a66c9d5dc776 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:35:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 11/17] Call callbacks through their own function types

Calling a function through a pointer to a type with a different
parameter list or return type is undefined behaviour. Most native ABIs
tolerate it; WebAssembly checks the type of every indirect call and
traps with "function signature mismatch".

Where GLib forwards a callback it was given, or one of its own
functions, to an iterator or notifier of another type, call it through
its own type instead: with a direct loop, a small wrapper, or a
trampoline that takes the real function as user data. These apply on
every platform and don't change behaviour.

- glist, gslist, gqueue, gasyncqueue: *_free_full() and
  g_async_queue_unref() called a GDestroyNotify as a GFunc.
- glist, gslist, garray, gtree: the GCompareFunc variants of sort,
  insert_sorted and g_tree_new() called it as a GCompareDataFunc.
- gtestutils: tests added with g_test_add_func(), g_test_add_data_func()
  and g_test_add_data_func_full() were called as GTestFixtureFunc, and
  the data's GDestroyNotify likewise.
- gobject: g_object_watch_closure() used g_object_ref()/unref() as
  GClosureNotify marshal guards; weak pointers used g_nullify_pointer()
  as a GWeakNotify (only replaced on WebAssembly, since callers may
  match that pair in g_object_weak_unref()).
- gbinding, gcancellable, gdbusserver, gdbusobjectmanagerclient: one-
  argument free functions, and g_cancellable_connect()'s
  data_destroy_func, used as GClosureNotify.
- gtestdbus: g_main_loop_quit() used as a GWeakNotify.
- gdbusintrospection, gdbusobjectmanagerclient, gdbusobjectproxy,
  gdbusobjectskeleton, gdbusprivate: ref/unref functions passed to
  g_list_foreach()/g_ptr_array_foreach() as GFunc.
- gsettingsbackend, gkeyfilesettingsbackend: strcmp() used as a
  GCompareDataFunc for GTree keys.
- gtypemodule: GTypeModule's class_init took one argument, so every GIO
  extension point (and any GTypeModule) trapped on first use.
- gbookmarkfile: g_bookmark_file_copy() passed g_strdup() and one-
  argument copy functions to g_list_copy_deep() as GCopyFunc.
- gsourceclosure: g_closure_ref(), which returns the closure, was the
  void-returning ref function of a closure source's callback funcs.
- gdbusinterfaceskeleton, gdbusobjectmanagerserver, gdbusobjectproxy,
  gdbusobjectskeleton, gmemorymonitorbase, and the interface init
  functions gdbus-codegen emits: one-argument GInterfaceInitFunc.
- gtester: g_free() passed to g_slist_foreach() as a GFunc.

G_DEFINE_DYNAMIC_TYPE_EXTENDED registers type_name##_init_adapter as the
instance init function but never defines it, so any code using
G_DEFINE_DYNAMIC_TYPE failed to compile. Define it, and register the
one-argument class_finalize through an adapter too, instead of calling
it as a two-argument GClassFinalizeFunc.

A signal's C marshaller calls a closure's callback with the instance,
the signal's parameters and a trailing user data pointer. Class
handlers - the functions in class structures that g_signal_new()
registers by offset, and those given to g_signal_new_class_handler()
and g_signal_override_class_handler() - never declare that pointer, so
on WebAssembly every emission that ran a class handler trapped, in
GLib's own classes and in every library built on GObject. On
WebAssembly, give the closures made for class handlers (those from
g_signal_type_cclosure_new(), g_signal_new_class_handler() and
g_signal_override_class_handler()) a marshaller of their own when they
are created: a variant of the libffi generic marshaller, and of its va
form, that leaves the user data out. Signals then keep the closure's
marshaller instead of installing theirs, so every emission of a class
closure, including chaining up with g_signal_chain_from_overridden()
and g_signal_chain_from_overridden_handler(), calls the handler with
exactly the instance and the signal's parameters. Closures connected as
handlers, or passed as class closures by the caller, are unchanged:
they may take user data.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
